# {{title}} {#v-top}

## A chart binds to data; it never holds any {#v-chart}

=== table {#v-facts caption="Revenue by segment" format=csv header=1}
Segment, Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4
Cloud,    8, 10, 12, 14
Platform, 5,  6,  7,  9
Services, 3,  4,  4,  5
===

=== view {#v-fy src=#v-facts caption="With the full-year column" compute="FY [%.0f] = Q1 + Q2 + Q3 + Q4"}
===

=== diagram {#v-bar format=geml-chart data=#v-fy type=bar x=Segment y=FY caption="FY by segment"}
===

=== diagram {#v-line format=geml-chart data=#v-facts type=line x=Segment y=Q4 caption="Q4 by segment"}
===

One source of truth, two pictures. The `y=FY` axis names a column a **view**
computes — the chart reads the derived relation, not the raw facts.

## Math {#v-math}

=== math {#v-pythagoras caption="Pythagoras"}
c^2 = a^2 + b^2
===

Inline works too: the discriminant is $b^2 - 4ac$.

## A diagram hosts a foreign DSL, untouched {#v-mermaid}

=== diagram {#v-flow format=mermaid caption="Review flow"}
graph LR
  A[Draft] --> B{Review} --> C[Publish]
===

GEML never interprets a `mermaid` body. It stores it verbatim and hands it to a
renderer — which is why adding a diagram language costs the format nothing.

## Except this one, which GEML does interpret {#v-codegraph}

`geml-code-graph` is not a foreign DSL. It points at a **codemap** — a call
graph stored as ordinary, checked GEML documents. The map below is this
repository's own source: the reference parser and the viewer that is drawing
this page. The two clusters touch only at the package boundary, because the
viewer calls the parser's published API and not its internals.

**Click a module to open its rendered page.**

=== diagram {#v-modmap format=geml-code-graph src=codemap/index.geml caption="geml-parser + geml-viewer — module overview (their own codemap)"}
===

And one container at method level, rooted at `renderHtml`. **Click a node for
its callee chain; the ⊕ on an entry node expands the caller chain up to the app
entry**, and the breadcrumb walks back without leaving the page. Dashed red is a
recursion back-edge, dotted is a dispatch candidate, dimmed is a leaf helper:

=== diagram {#v-callmap format=geml-code-graph src=codemap/geml-parser--render.ts.geml caption="geml-parser/src/render.ts — layered method flow (interactive)"}
===
